en: '<p class="l-text"><strong>Indexing and slicing are how you talk to NumPy arrays.</strong> Creating arrays is only the beginning — the real power comes from selecting, filtering, and reshaping data within them. Whether you are extracting a single pixel from an image, selecting all negative values in a dataset, or grabbing a batch of training samples for your neural network, you need to master NumPy\'s indexing system.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text">This lesson covers every indexing technique you will encounter in real ML/NLP work: basic indexing, slicing, boolean masks, fancy indexing, structured selection, and real-world patterns for images and batches. Each concept builds on the previous one, so follow in order.</p>'
+ '<div class="lesson-outcomes" style="background:rgba(200,169,110,0.06);border-left:3px solid #c8a96e;padding:1rem 1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0"><div style="font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.08em;color:#c8a96e;margin-bottom:0.6rem">📍 WHAT YOU\'LL LEARN</div><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.3rem;line-height:1.65;font-size:0.93rem;color:rgba(235,230,220,0.92)"><li>Index 1D and 2D arrays with integers, slices, and negative offsets</li><li>Apply boolean masks to filter elements based on conditions</li><li>Use fancy indexing with integer arrays to gather scattered elements</li><li>Distinguish views from copies and avoid silent in-place mutation bugs</li><li>Combine slicing and broadcasting to assign values to subarrays</li></ul></div>'


/* ============================================================
   SECTION 1: Basic Indexing
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">1. Basic Indexing</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Everyday analogy:</strong> Think of a NumPy array as a bookshelf. A 1D array is a single shelf — you grab a book by its position number. A 2D array is a grid of shelves — you need a row number AND a column number. A 3D array is a library with multiple rooms, each containing a grid of shelves — now you need room, row, and column.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">NumPy uses <strong>zero-based indexing</strong>, just like Python lists. The first element is at index 0, the second at index 1, and so on. Negative indices count from the end: -1 is the last element, -2 is second-to-last.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards"><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">1D Indexing</div><div class="card-body">Access the element at position i. a[0] is the first element, a[-1] is the last.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">2D Indexing</div><div class="card-body">Access row i, column j. Equivalent to a[i][j] but faster — NumPy handles it in one step.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">3D Indexing</div><div class="card-body">Access depth i, row j, column k. Think: which "layer", which row, which column.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Negative Index</div><div class="card-body">Count from the end. -1 = last, -2 = second-to-last. Works in every dimension.</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">WORKED EXAMPLE</div><div class="example-body"><strong>1D indexing:</strong><br>arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]<br>arr[0] = 10 (first element)<br>arr[3] = 40 (fourth element)<br>arr[-1] = 50 (last element)<br>arr[-2] = 40 (second-to-last)<br><br><strong>2D indexing:</strong><br>matrix = [[1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8,9]]<br>matrix[0,0] = 1 (top-left corner)<br>matrix[1,2] = 6 (row 1, col 2)<br>matrix[-1,-1] = 9 (bottom-right corner)<br>matrix[2] = [7,8,9] (entire row 2)</div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># --- 1D Indexing ---</span>\narr = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">20</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">40</span>, <span class="num">50</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(arr[<span class="num">0</span>])    <span class="cm"># 10 — first element</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(arr[<span class="num">-1</span>])   <span class="cm"># 50 — last element</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(arr[<span class="num">-2</span>])   <span class="cm"># 40 — second to last</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># --- 2D Indexing ---</span>\nmat = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>],\n                [<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">6</span>],\n                [<span class="num">7</span>, <span class="num">8</span>, <span class="num">9</span>]])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">0</span>])   <span class="cm"># 1   — top-left</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>])   <span class="cm"># 6   — row 1, col 2</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[<span class="num">-1</span>, <span class="num">-1</span>]) <span class="cm"># 9   — bottom-right</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[<span class="num">2</span>])       <span class="cm"># [7 8 9] — entire row 2</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[:, <span class="num">1</span>])    <span class="cm"># [2 5 8] — entire column 1</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># --- 3D Indexing ---</span>\ncube = np.<span class="fn">arange</span>(<span class="num">24</span>).<span class="fn">reshape</span>(<span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(cube.shape)       <span class="cm"># (2, 3, 4)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(cube[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>])   <span class="cm"># element at depth 0, row 1, col 2</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(cube[<span class="num">1</span>])          <span class="cm"># shape (3,4) — entire 2nd "layer"</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(cube[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">2</span>])       <span class="cm"># shape (4,) — row 2 of layer 0</span></code></pre></div>'

