+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. Slicing</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Everyday analogy:</strong> Slicing is like cutting a piece from a loaf of bread. You say "start here, stop there, and take every n-th slice." The syntax start:stop:step works identically to Python lists, but NumPy slicing is much more powerful because it works across multiple dimensions simultaneously.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">The general syntax is <code>array[start:stop:step]</code>. Like Python, <strong>start is inclusive, stop is exclusive</strong>. Omitting start defaults to 0, omitting stop defaults to the array length, and omitting step defaults to 1.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards"><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Basic Slice</div><div class="card-body">Elements from index 1 up to (not including) 4. Returns a[1], a[2], a[3].</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Step Slice</div><div class="card-body">Every 2nd element from beginning to end. a[::3] gives every 3rd element.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Reverse</div><div class="card-body">Negative step reverses the array. a[::-1] is the full array reversed.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Full Form</div><div class="card-body">Start at 1, stop before 5, step by 2. Returns a[1] and a[3].</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-title">VIEWS vs COPIES — Critical Difference</div><div class="compare-left"><div class="compare-label">VIEW (Slicing)</div>A slice returns a <strong>view</strong> of the original array. Modifying the slice modifies the original!<br><br><code>b = a[1:4]</code><br><code>b[0] = 999</code><br>Now a[1] is also 999.<br><br>Views share the same memory — no data is copied. This is fast but dangerous if you do not understand it.</div><div class="compare-right"><div class="compare-label">COPY (Explicit)</div>Use <code>.copy()</code> to create an independent copy.<br><br><code>b = a[1:4].copy()</code><br><code>b[0] = 999</code><br>a[1] is unchanged.<br><br>Copies allocate new memory. Safer but uses more RAM. Always copy when you need independence.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\narr = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">20</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">40</span>, <span class="num">50</span>, <span class="num">60</span>, <span class="num">70</span>, <span class="num">80</span>])\n\n<span class="cm"># Basic slicing</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(arr[<span class="num">2</span>:<span class="num">5</span>])     <span class="cm"># [30 40 50]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(arr[:<span class="num">3</span>])      <span class="cm"># [10 20 30]     — first 3</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(arr[<span class="num">5</span>:])      <span class="cm"># [60 70 80]     — from index 5</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(arr[::<span class="num">2</span>])     <span class="cm"># [10 30 50 70]  — every 2nd</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(arr[::<span class="num">-1</span>])    <span class="cm"># [80 70 60 50 40 30 20 10] — reversed</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># 2D slicing</span>\nmat = np.<span class="fn">arange</span>(<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">17</span>).<span class="fn">reshape</span>(<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">4</span>)\n<span class="cm"># [[ 1  2  3  4]</span>\n<span class="cm">#  [ 5  6  7  8]</span>\n<span class="cm">#  [ 9 10 11 12]</span>\n<span class="cm">#  [13 14 15 16]]</span>\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[<span class="num">1</span>:<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">1</span>:<span class="num">3</span>])  <span class="cm"># [[ 6  7] [10 11]] — center 2x2</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[:<span class="num">2</span>, :])       <span class="cm"># first 2 rows, all columns</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[:, <span class="num">-1</span>])       <span class="cm"># [4 8 12 16] — last column</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[::<span class="num">2</span>, ::<span class="num">2</span>])   <span class="cm"># [[ 1  3] [ 9 11]] — every other row & col</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># VIEW vs COPY — critical!</span>\nslice_view = mat[<span class="num">0</span>:<span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">0</span>:<span class="num">2</span>]\nslice_view[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">0</span>] = <span class="num">999</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">0</span>])  <span class="cm"># 999! Original was modified</span>\n\nslice_copy = mat[<span class="num">2</span>:<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">2</span>:<span class="num">4</span>].<span class="fn">copy</span>()\nslice_copy[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">0</span>] = <span class="num">-1</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[<span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">2</span>])  <span class="cm"># 11 — original unchanged</span></code></pre></div>'

