/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: Boolean Indexing
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Boolean Indexing</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Everyday analogy:</strong> Boolean indexing is like a filter on a spreadsheet. You define a condition — "show me all rows where sales > 1000" — and the filter shows only the matching entries. In NumPy, the condition creates a True/False mask, and True positions get selected.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">When you apply a comparison operator to a NumPy array, you get a <strong>boolean array</strong> of the same shape. Using this boolean array as an index returns only the elements where the condition is True.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards"><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Condition Mask</div><div class="card-body">arr > 5 produces a boolean array: [False, False, True, True, ...]. Each element is compared independently.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">AND Mask</div><div class="card-body">(arr > 2) & (arr < 8) — both conditions must be True. Use parentheses around each condition!</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">OR Mask</div><div class="card-body">(arr < 2) | (arr > 8) — at least one condition must be True.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">NOT Mask</div><div class="card-body">~(arr > 5) — inverts the mask. True becomes False and vice versa.</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">WORKED EXAMPLE</div><div class="example-body"><strong>Array:</strong> data = [3, 7, 1, 9, 4, 6, 2, 8]<br><br><strong>Mask:</strong> data > 5 = [False, True, False, True, False, True, False, True]<br><strong>Result:</strong> data[data > 5] = [7, 9, 6, 8]<br><br><strong>Combined:</strong> (data > 3) & (data < 8) = [False, True, False, False, True, True, False, False]<br><strong>Result:</strong> data[(data > 3) & (data < 8)] = [7, 4, 6]<br><br><strong>np.where:</strong> np.where(data > 5, data, 0) = [0, 7, 0, 9, 0, 6, 0, 8]<br>Keeps values where True, replaces with 0 where False.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\ndata = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">7</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">9</span>, <span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">6</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">8</span>])\n\n<span class="cm"># Basic boolean mask</span>\nmask = data > <span class="num">5</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mask)       <span class="cm"># [False  True False  True False  True False  True]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(data[mask]) <span class="cm"># [7 9 6 8]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Combined conditions — parentheses required!</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(data[(data > <span class="num">3</span>) & (data < <span class="num">8</span>)])  <span class="cm"># [7 4 6]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(data[(data < <span class="num">3</span>) | (data > <span class="num">7</span>)])  <span class="cm"># [1 9 2 8]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(data[~(data > <span class="num">5</span>)])              <span class="cm"># [3 1 4 2] — NOT greater than 5</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># np.where — conditional replacement</span>\nresult = np.<span class="fn">where</span>(data > <span class="num">5</span>, data, <span class="num">0</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)  <span class="cm"># [0 7 0 9 0 6 0 8]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># np.where for finding indices</span>\nindices = np.<span class="fn">where</span>(data > <span class="num">5</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(indices)  <span class="cm"># (array([1, 3, 5, 7]),)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># 2D boolean indexing</span>\nmat = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>],\n                [<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">6</span>],\n                [<span class="num">7</span>, <span class="num">8</span>, <span class="num">9</span>]])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(mat[mat > <span class="num">5</span>])    <span class="cm"># [6 7 8 9] — always returns 1D!</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Practical: clip outliers</span>\nscores = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">85</span>, <span class="num">200</span>, <span class="num">72</span>, <span class="num">-5</span>, <span class="num">91</span>, <span class="num">150</span>])\nscores[scores > <span class="num">100</span>] = <span class="num">100</span>   <span class="cm"># cap at 100</span>\nscores[scores < <span class="num">0</span>] = <span class="num">0</span>       <span class="cm"># floor at 0</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(scores)  <span class="cm"># [85 100  72   0  91 100]</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>ML Connection:</strong> Boolean indexing is critical in data preprocessing. Removing NaN values: <code>data[~np.isnan(data)]</code>. Filtering by label: <code>X[y == 1]</code> gives all samples of class 1. In attention mechanisms, boolean masks prevent attending to padding tokens: <code>attention[mask == 0] = -1e9</code>.</div>'

/* --- Plotly: boolean mask visualization --- */
